# Engineering rules (pstack)

Work from constraints. Ship the smallest change that can be verified. Do not invent architecture for a future that is not in the request.

## Constraints first

Write down what this change must not do before writing code. In this repo that always includes:

- One PR = one scope. Do not mix scrape, RSS, cache, mobile, SEO, MCP, or dependency bumps.
- Frontend is a static Next.js export (`output: 'export'`). No SSR-only features.
- Never commit `.env`, secrets, or API keys. TMDB stays on the backend. Supabase uses the anon key only.
- ZIP export is the guaranteed fallback when the residential worker is offline. Username HTML scrape runs only on that worker, never on Render.
- Do not merge PRs unless the owner explicitly asked to merge in this turn.
- Do not revert merged PRs unless the owner explicitly asked.

## Dead systems — do not resurrect

These were built, operated, and then removed on purpose. Do not restore them unless the owner names them in the request:

- Windows desktop worker and its PowerShell supervisor (`desktop_scrape_worker.py`, `backend/start-worker*.ps1`)
- HTML scraping on Render or any other datacenter IP
- Paid/proxy scrape (ScraperAPI and any successor)
- Watchlist-compare / Date Night / FindFilm scrape jobs
- Browser-use / Hermes as a general scraper

The chosen username-scrape path is the residential Linux worker on the X370: outbound poll, curl_cffi Safari impersonation, one job at a time, `WORKER_TOKEN`. That work is PR #150 (`cursor/letterboxd-username-scrape-196f`). Do not forbid it, and do not replace it with a second worker design. It is not live until that backend is deployed and the X370 is running the same git sha.

`docs/desktop-worker-*` describes the retired Windows worker. It is not the X370 control plane. The X370 doc is `docs/x370-safari-worker.md` on the PR #150 branch. `archive/scrape` is the old snapshot, not the chosen path.

If a task seems to need a forbidden path above, stop and report. Do not "just scrape on Render" or "just try a proxy."

## Small verified changes

- Read the file you will edit. Change one behavior. Keep the diff small.
- Prove the behavior with a test or a real run of the affected path before calling it done.
- Frontend: `cd frontend && npx tsc --noEmit` plus the relevant vitest files.
- Backend: `cd backend && python -m pytest` for the tests that cover the change (full suite before claiming done).
- If you cannot verify UI in a browser, say what you ran instead. Do not claim visual verification from a screenshot alone.

## Tests

- New behavior needs a test that would fail without the change.
- Do not delete or skip a test to make a scrape/proxy path look green.
- Prefers fixtures and hermetic tests over live Letterboxd hits.

## No speculative abstraction

- Do not add helpers, layers, feature flags, or "for later" modules.
- Do not extract a shared interface until a second real caller exists in this PR.
- Do not rebuild a second worker, a different job queue, or an RSS platform "so scrape can scale later." The X370 residential worker is the chosen path.
- Duplicate a ten-line helper before introducing a new package.

## Default move when blocked

If Cloudflare or a Render IP is the blocker: do not scrape from Render, and do not add a proxy or a datacenter browser. ZIP remains the fallback. The residential X370 worker is the chosen scrape path; do not invent another one.
